# A GENUINE `skip 3` — the number a user writes for their own raw download.
#
# `preamble.csv` opens with a title line and an account line before its header,
# so three records have to go before the first transaction. That is the whole
# point of this file: the number is counted against the file the BANK produced,
# and Ledgeline's conversion strips the preamble out from under it.
#
# Run against our converted CSV — header on line 1 — hledger spends its three
# skips on the header and the first two transactions, and imports the third one
# alone: ONE of three, exit code 0, nothing on stderr. Verified against hledger
# 1.52. (Two rows or fewer and the same arithmetic reads zero.)
# `convert::align_to_skip` is what puts the two frames back together, and
# `converted_and_aligned_imports_what_the_raw_download_does` in
# crates/ledgeline-core/tests/convert_tabular.rs is what proves it with this
# pair.
#
# Everything below `skip` is ordinary and deliberately so — a `currency` so the
# amounts are in `$` rather than the bare commodity, a `date-format` that reads
# this file's ISO dates — because the only thing being tested here is the
# record-counting frame.

skip 3
fields date, description, amount, balance
date-format %Y-%m-%d
currency $

account1 assets:bank:checking
account2 expenses:unknown
